  {% entry "PhD Computer Science – Indiana University, IN, USA", "2015 – 2021", "edu-phd-indiana" %}
    <p>
      <b>Dissertation:</b> <a href="{{ site.links.dissertation }}">Self-Hosting Functional Programming Languages on Meta-Tracing JIT Compilers</a>
    </p>
    <p>
      Specialized in Programming Languages (PL) under the supervision of <a href="http://homes.sice.indiana.edu/samth/">Sam Tobin‑Hochstadt</a>, minored in Logic at the <a href="http://www.philosophy.indiana.edu/areas/ll.shtml">Department of Philosophy</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
      My work focuses on fast run‑times of dynamic languages. In the first phase of my research, I investigated the effects of using Racket byte‑code compiler as a frontend for Pycket--studying optimizations like heavy inlining and lambda lifting and their effects on Pycket's run-time.
    </p>
    <p>
      When Racket became more portable and <a href="https://blog.racket-lang.org/2018/01/racket-on-chez-status.html">started adopting Chez Scheme as its primary run time</a>, I pivoted to help that effort. We had a rudimentary Racket implementation, <a href="https://github.com/pycket/pycket">Pycket</a>, a meta‑tracing JIT compiler built on <a href="https://rpython.readthedocs.io/en/latest/">RPython framework</a>. It was designed to demonstrate that <a href="https://github.com/pycket/pycket/tree/master/papers">efficient sound gradual typing is possible</a>. I helped make Racket more portable and adopt Chez Scheme (see <a href="#pub-racket-on-chez">publication</a>). Mimicking Racket’s self‑hosting on Racket‑on‑Chez, I turned Pycket into a full implementation of Racket with a meta‑tracing JIT as its run‑time.
    </p>
    <p>
      <a href="https://pypy.org/">PyPy</a> showed that fast prototyping of efficient run‑times for dynamic languages is possible through meta‑interpretation. My work takes this a step further to show that fast prototyping of efficient run‑times for <strong>self‑hosting</strong> dynamic languages is achievable.
    </p>
    <p>
      Currently <a href="https://github.com/pycket/pycket">Pycket</a> works as a runtime for full self-hosting Racket, including green threads via engines and full rktio via ffi--essentially no different than Racket on Chez.
    </p>
    <p>
      My <a href="{{ site.links.dissertation }}">dissertation</a> contains all the tecnical details on how this is achieved for the first time ever, along with a full performance investigation of self-hosting a production-grade language on a tracing JIT compiler.
    </p>
